# Diagnosing a native E2E failure

Written after a 12-hour diagnosis session on 2026-08-28/29 that should have taken
twenty minutes. Every rule here is a scar. Follow them in order.

## 1. Run it locally FIRST. CI is for confirmation, never for diagnosis.

```bash
scripts/e2e-native-local.sh --platform ios
```

A local iOS run of flows 01–06 takes **~5 minutes**. The CI round trip is
**~60 minutes**. That is a 12x difference, and on 2026-08-28 an entire night was
spent on the 60-minute loop for a bug the 5-minute loop showed immediately.

The dev client contains no JS — Metro serves the bundle — so a local run tests the
working tree with no build and no EAS spend. There is no reason to use CI as the
first look.

**Do not open a CI log to diagnose a failure you have not tried to reproduce
locally.** If it does not reproduce locally, that is itself the most valuable
finding you will get all day: it means the bug is environmental, and you should
be reading the runner's differences, not the app's code.

## 2. Read the app's errors BEFORE the Maestro assertion message.

Maestro reports what it could not find. It cannot report *why*, and the two are
routinely unrelated.

The canonical failure of this suite looks like:

```
[Failed] 03-authenticated-relaunch (2m 4s)
   (Assertion is false: id: search-products is visible)
```

which reads as "the app never launched" or "the search screen is broken or
slow". On 2026-08-29 (run 33241496921) it was **neither**: the app was fully
loaded — POS grid rendered, 209 products, cart, customer — with a **red error
overlay** sitting on top of `search-products`. Maestro waited out its whole
120-second budget looking at a working app behind a red box.

So:

- **`<element> is visible FAILED` is a SYMPTOM, not a cause.** Before theorising
  about launch time, sync speed, or a missing testID, establish whether anything
  was covering the screen.
- The app errors are already captured. In CI: the **job summary** (`App errors
  logged during this run`), the `::warning::` annotations, and the
  `app-errors-*` artifact. Locally: printed first by
  `scripts/e2e-native-local.sh`. **Look there before you look at the flow.**
- A red box means a `console.error`. Under `--no-dev` the dev client still draws
  it — `LogBox.ignoreAllLogs()` does NOT suppress it (it is `// Do nothing.` in
  the non-`__DEV__` branch of RN's `LogBox.js`), so do not "fix" this by trying
  to silence LogBox.

## 3. Green is not healthy. Check the measurement, not just the verdict.

The single most expensive habit in this whole episode was **treating a tool's
verdict as the truth about the system**. Maestro prints a verdict and a duration
in the same string; only one of them was read.

Run 33243418607 reported:

```
[Passed] 03-authenticated-relaunch (45m 53s)
```

That flow's healthy time is **14-45 seconds**. A sixty-fold overrun, marked
green, sitting next to a `::warning::` that nobody read — while the diagnosis
effort went to the row labelled `[Failed]`.

- **Compare every measurement to a physical expectation, not to a configured
  threshold.** A cashier taps a search box and sees results in about a second.
  So a *step* measured in minutes is a defect at 60x whatever the verdict says.
  A threshold encodes somebody's past guess; a physical expectation encodes
  reality. If you print a number, say what it should be.
- **A pass with an absurd measurement is a failure.** The verdict and the
  duration are two independent pieces of evidence.
- This is now a **gate**, not a habit: the timing triage fails the job above
  1200s (`ABSURD`) and warns above 600s (`STARVED`), on both platforms. Do not
  weaken it back to a warning — a warning is what let 45m53s pass.

The same reflex explains the rest of the series: `<element> is visible FAILED`
was accepted as "the element is missing" rather than "what states of the world
produce this observation?"; a job that exited 0 was accepted as a job that
passed (#1662). **The label outranked the evidence every time.**

## 4. One screenshot is the final frame, not the story.

Maestro saves a single screenshot at the moment of failure. Everything before it
is invisible, so a diagnosis built on it alone is backwards inference — and it is
easy to misread a still (the same frame was misread twice in one night: once as a
dev-launcher home screen, once as a slow search).

`scripts/e2e-native-local.sh` records the whole run to `screen.mp4` by default.
**Watch the video before forming a theory.**

## 5. A flow that fails only in CI is usually missing fixtures, not broken.

Flows that assert on seeded records (currently 07 and anything else using
`VARIABLE_PRODUCT_*`) need `scripts/e2e-native-seed.mjs`, which needs
`E2E_PRODUCT_WRITER_USER` / `E2E_PRODUCT_WRITER_PASS`. Without them the local run
warns and those flows fail on missing data, which says nothing about the product.
Set the credentials locally before concluding anything about a seeded flow.

## 6. Fix the class at the throw site, not the instance at the catch site.

Three separate red boxes in one week were the same class: **a cancellation that
the classifier could not recognise as one.**

- expo's winter fetch rejects an abort with a plain `Error` wrapping
  `FetchRequestCanceledException`, not a WHATWG `AbortError` (#1674).
- React Native's `AbortController` is the `abort-controller` polyfill, whose
  `abort()` **takes no arguments** — an abort *reason* passed to it is silently
  discarded, so `signal.reason` is `undefined` on native and only on native
  (#1677).

Each was first "fixed" by adding another string to a regex in the catch. That is
an instance fix, and the next variant walks straight past it. **Name the error
correctly where it is thrown** (`Object.assign(new Error(msg), { name:
'AbortError' })`) so every downstream classifier inherits it.

See `packages/sync-engine/src/maintenance/variation-prefetch.ts` for the pattern.

## 7. Prefer a unit test over a device test for platform-seam bugs.

Every native-only bug in this series was a **platform fact** — what RN's
`AbortController` does with a reason, what expo's fetch names an abort, whether
`DOMException` is a global. Those are testable in milliseconds, and were instead
found by hour-long device runs.

`packages/sync-engine/src/platform-seam.test.ts` pins these facts. When you find
a new native/web divergence, **add it there** so the next one is caught by
`vitest`, not by Maestro.

## 8. Cost discipline

Native E2E builds are metered — $2 iOS / $1 Android against a shared $45/month
credit. **Never dispatch `e2e-native.yml` with `build=true` without asking the
owner.** JS-only changes never need a build; a dispatch defaults to
`build=false` and fails fast on a cache miss. See the root `CLAUDE.md`.
